-- Migration: Sheet Metal Lookup Tables -- Nesting Cut Rate, batch 1 of N (staging)
-- Purpose: Lands the "sheetMetalNestingCutRate" export, batch 1 (569 rows,
--          source itself flags this as PARTIAL -- more batches to come) into
--          the same lossless staging table earlier Lookup Tables migrations
--          created. See migration 479's header for the staging/promotion
--          architecture.
--
--          This is the richest laser-cutting dataset seen in this whole
--          reconciliation effort -- REAL gas-type-specific (Nitrogen vs
--          Oxygen assist) feed rates split by large-feature vs small-feature
--          geometry, well beyond this app's current sm_lookup_laser_cut
--          schema (single cutting_speed_m_per_min per material/thickness/
--          power, no gas type, no feature-size split). Breakdown:
--            - Fiber Laser Cut, Steel: 140 rows (31 Nitrogen + 109 Oxygen) --
--              directly relevant to this app's real fiber_laser engine.
--            - "Laser Cut" (no gas given), Steel: 112 rows, power levels
--              include non-round figures (2700W/3200W/3300W) matching this
--              app's real CO2-laser-class machines -- almost certainly CO2
--              laser data, relevant to the co2_laser engine.
--            - Plasma Cut (130 rows) + OxyFuel Cut (102 rows), Steel: NO
--              consumer -- this app has zero cost engine for either process
--              (confirmed earlier in this reconciliation: bom-items.service.ts
--              explicitly cites Plasma Cutting as an example of "no
--              registered ManufacturingProcessEngine").
--            - "Laser Cut", Cast Iron: 85 rows -- Cast Iron is not a material
--              this app's raw_materials catalog or classifiers handle at all.
--
--          NOT promoted into sm_lookup_laser_cut here -- doing so needs a
--          real schema change (add gas_type + split feed rate by feature
--          size) and the source itself says more batches (more material
--          families) are coming. Promoting now risks a second schema
--          migration once the fuller picture arrives. See project memory
--          project_manufacturing_intelligence_data_reconciliation.md.
